# Documented differences between the built-in YAML parser and libyaml,
# read by fuzz_yamldiff (card N-12). One rule a line, three fields
# separated by tabs: the side (lib-refuses, bi-refuses or events), a key,
# and the reason: which reading is right, and why. A reason that starts
# with "to fix" is a difference that is a bug, not a decision.
#
# The key of a refusal (lib-refuses: libyaml refuses; bi-refuses: the
# built-in parser refuses) is a part, 8 bytes at least, of the message of
# the side that refuses: one rule is one reason for refusing. The key of
# events is a byte string, 3 bytes at least, that the input contains.
# Escapes: \n \t \\ and \xHH.
lib-refuses	control characters are not allowed	to fix: YAML 1.2 allows no NUL or other control byte (c-printable) and libyaml refuses one anywhere; the built-in parser refuses it in a scalar but skips it in a comment (yamlparse.c yp_line_rest and yp_skip drop the result of cf_text_comment, whose body check is the TOML rule, which ends a comment at LF only)
bi-refuses	flow collection's line is not indented enough	the built-in parser is right: a flow collection inside a block mapping continues on lines indented more than the mapping (YAML 1.2 7.4, s-flow-line-prefix); libyaml accepts a continuation line at the mapping's own column
lib-refuses	did not find expected alphabetic or numeric character	the built-in parser is right: YAML 1.2 lets an anchor or alias name hold any non-space character but a flow indicator (ns-anchor-char, 6.9.2), `!` and `~` among them; libyaml keeps the 1.1 rule, letters, digits, `_` and `-`
lib-refuses	did not find expected node content	mostly the built-in parser is right: a plain scalar in a flow collection may start with `:` when a non-space follows (YAML 1.2 ns-plain-first, 7.3.3), which libyaml refuses; the message is libyaml's catch-all for a missing node, so the rule also hides a refusal of another cause with it (to narrow when one shows)
lib-refuses	cannot start any token	the built-in parser is right where a TAB is white space (s-white, YAML 1.2 6.2, a line of a tab and nothing else); libyaml refuses a TAB at the start of a token. The message is libyaml's for any byte that starts no token, so the rule also hides another cause with it (to narrow when one shows)
bi-refuses	quoted value's next line is not indented enough	the built-in parser is right: the lines of a quoted scalar inside a block mapping are indented more than the mapping (YAML 1.2 7.3.1, s-flow-line-prefix); libyaml accepts a continuation line at a lesser column
lib-refuses	found unexpected ':'	probably the built-in parser is right: in a flow sequence `a:` before a flow indicator is a single pair with an empty value (YAML 1.2 7.4.2, ns-flow-pair); libyaml refuses a `:` there. Not checked against a third parser; the message is libyaml's for a stray `:`, so the rule hides other causes with it (to narrow when one shows)
events	: |\n	to fix, or to decide: a literal block scalar whose last line holds only spaces and no line break after it ends in a line feed here (yamlparse.c, 'the input's end ends the last line too') and without one in libyaml; YAML 1.2 8.1.3 clipping keeps the final break only if there is one. Input in the card's notes. The key covers every literal scalar, so the rule hides any other difference in one (to narrow)
lib-refuses	mapping values are not allowed in this context	the built-in parser is right where the cause is an anchor name that ends in `:` (`&K0:` is the name K0: in YAML 1.2, ns-anchor-char), the same cause as the anchor rule above, which libyaml reports as a stray mapping value; the message is libyaml's for any `:` it cannot place, so the rule also hides another cause with it (to narrow when one shows)
bi-refuses	a value cannot start with this character	the built-in parser is right where the character is a `#` straight after a flow `,` or `[`: a comment needs white space before it (YAML 1.2 6.6, c-nb-comment-text after s-separate-in-line) and a plain scalar cannot start with `#`; libyaml reads the comment. The message is the built-in parser's for any indicator that starts no value, so the rule also hides another cause with it (to narrow when one shows)
bi-refuses	unexpected text after the value	the built-in parser is right where the text is a `#` straight after a closing quote or a flow collection: a comment needs white space before it (YAML 1.2 6.6); libyaml reads the comment. The message is the built-in parser's for any text after a complete value, so the rule also hides another cause with it (to narrow when one shows)
bi-refuses	empty line before the content is indented	the built-in parser is right: a leading empty line of a block scalar may not be more indented than the first content line (YAML 1.2 8.1.1.1); libyaml accepts it
bi-refuses	expected a key and ':'	to fix, or to decide: cause not examined (the card stopped at ten rules). The built-in parser refuses `motd : \n| \n  two\nbase{opy:` (a block scalar header at the mapping's own column, then text); libyaml accepts. The message is the built-in parser's for any line that is neither key nor value, so the rule hides other causes with it
lib-refuses	an empty key	the built-in parser is right where the cause is an anchor name that ends in `:` (`&d:` is the name d: in YAML 1.2), as in the anchor rules above; libyaml takes the `:` for a value indicator. The message is libyaml's for any key it finds empty, so the rule hides another cause with it (to narrow when one shows)
lib-refuses	tab character that violates indentation	the built-in parser is right where the TAB is white space after the indentation, or on a line with nothing else (s-white, YAML 1.2 6.2); libyaml refuses a TAB at the start of a line it takes for indentation. A TAB inside the indentation is refused by both and is not here
bi-refuses	a property is followed by white space	to decide: an anchor or tag followed by a TAB or a line break before its node; the input in the card's notes. The built-in parser refuses, libyaml accepts; which is right depends on whether the name here ends before the `:` (a key's anchor) or includes it (an anchor name, YAML 1.2), and the card stopped before looking
bi-refuses	block scalar's header is its indicators and a comment	the built-in parser is right where a `#` follows the indicators (`|#`): a comment needs white space before it (YAML 1.2 8.1.1 and 6.6); libyaml reads the comment. Found 2026-11-28. The message is the parser's for any text after the indicators, so the rule hides other causes too (to narrow when one shows)
